</div>
      <p>On August 12, 2026, Waninger filed a gender-discrimination lawsuit against Marathon Engineering & Environmental Services Inc. in New Jersey state court. The Fireflies transcript is her primary evidence.</p>
      <p>The alleged remark is quoted from the complaint as reported by Law360, and reproduced by Ford & Harrison attorney Mark Saloman in an analysis published on Mondaq. Those two accounts are the whole record available outside the courthouse. The complaint itself is not publicly accessible online, the two sources may derive from a single reading of it, and no independent third account of its contents was identified.</p>
      <p>One layer of mediation matters here. The transcript Waninger received is not a verbatim recording. Saloman states explicitly that it is a "non-verbatim AI-generated summary." The alleged remark passed through a model's interpretation before it reached the plaintiff. Whether the AI's paraphrase is a reliable representation of what was said is itself an open question — one the case will need to resolve.</p>
      <p>Marathon Engineering has not responded to the allegations. The complaint is seven days old and unanswered. In New Jersey state court, defendants typically have 35 days after service to file an answer.</p>
    </section>

  </div>

  <div class="phantom-wrap">
    <canvas class="ai-notetaker-termination-transcript-canvas" aria-hidden="true"></canvas>
  </div>
  <p class="canvas-note"><strong>The Emission</strong> — A recording node pulses alone after its surrounding meeting cluster goes dark one by one; its signal fans out to every original position, where one node absorbs the evidence and another recoils from the violation.</p>

  <div class="container">

    <section class="section" id="s02">
      <h2><span class="num">02 — MECHANISM</span>The Defaults</h2>
      <p>Three factory settings in Fireflies' product documentation explain how the transcript was generated and delivered. Each is confirmed by the manufacturer's own knowledge base.</p>
      <p><strong>Auto-join.</strong> By default, the Fireflies bot joins every calendar event that has a web conference link. No per-meeting approval is required. If the meeting is on the calendar and has a Zoom, Teams, or Google Meet URL, the bot attends.</p>
      <p><strong>Email recap.</strong> By default, Fireflies sends the completed transcript to "everyone on the invite" — every person on the original calendar invitation, internal or external. The distribution is not limited to the bot's owner or the meeting organizer.</p>
      <p><strong>Session duration.</strong> The bot stays in a meeting for up to two to four hours, depending on the user's plan. It waits ten to fifteen minutes for a meeting to start before leaving. The documentation describes the session in terms of elapsed time only — it says nothing about tracking who is still in the room.</p>
<div class="nt-defaults">
