    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="nt-defaults-footer">Source: Fireflies.ai product documentation. These are the factory settings — no administrator action required.</div>
</div>
      <p>These are not settings an administrator configured for the Waninger meeting. They are the factory state. The gap between "I added a productivity tool" and "I created a permanent, automatically distributed record of every meeting" is zero configuration steps.</p>
      <p>Two links in the chain are inferences rather than record. The bot appears to have kept recording after Waninger left the call — the transcript captured remarks made after her departure, and the documentation confirms the bot stays for up to four hours — but no source states that this default was in effect for this meeting. The same gap applies to delivery: the default sends transcripts to all calendar invitees, and Waninger received a link, but whether that was the default firing or someone manually triggering the share is not documented anywhere.</p>
      <p>This failure mode is not new. In September 2024, ML engineer Alex Bilzerian had a Zoom meeting with a venture capital firm that used Otter AI. After the meeting ended, the Otter bot stayed on the line, recorded several more hours of the VCs' private conversation — including discussion of what Bilzerian described as "strategic failures and cooked metrics" — and emailed the transcript to all participants, including Bilzerian. His post on X about the incident reached over five million views. Otter CEO Sam Liang's response: "If you want AI to help, you need to put AI in the meetings."</p>
      <p>Different vendor. Different year. Same mechanical sequence: the bot persisted after participants believed the meeting was over, captured conversation the speakers assumed was private, and distributed the transcript to someone who was not meant to see it.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="s03">
      <h2><span class="num">03 — THE DOCKET</span>Four Courtrooms</h2>
      <p>While the Waninger transcript was being generated, four separate lawsuits were working their way through federal courts, each targeting AI notetaker vendors for recording without consent. The complaints are different. The default behaviors at issue are the same.</p>
      <p><strong>In re Otter.AI Privacy Litigation</strong> (No. 5:25-cv-06911, N.D. Cal.). Filed August 15, 2025. Four class actions consolidated before Judge Eumi K. Lee. The plaintiffs allege Otter's bot joins meetings uninvited, records without meaningful consent, and transmits recordings to Otter's servers. A motion-to-dismiss hearing was held May 20, 2026; the ruling is under submission.</p>
      <p><strong>Cruz v. Fireflies.AI Corp.</strong> (C.D. Ill.). Filed December 18, 2025. The plaintiff alleges Fireflies collected biometric voiceprints during virtual meetings without written consent, in violation of the Illinois Biometric Information Privacy Act. This case, too, traced to a single account — one published by a company that competes with Fireflies.</p>
      <p><strong>Basich et al. v. Microsoft Corporation</strong> (No. 2:26-cv-00422, W.D. Wash.). Filed February 5, 2026 by five Illinois residents who allege Microsoft Teams' live transcription feature creates biometric voiceprints in violation of BIPA. Unlike the other three, this case was traced to only one account — an analysis published by a litigation-funding platform — so the docket number and the filing details rest on a single source.</p>
      <p><strong>Chamberlain v. Granola, Inc.</strong> (No. 3:26-cv-07926, N.D. Cal.). Filed July 30, 2026. The plaintiff alleges Granola — a notetaker that markets itself as "bot-free" — secretly records conversations and uses them to train its AI models.</p>
      <p>The common thread: the tools record too much, distribute too freely, and do not obtain adequate consent. The complaint in each case is about the default behavior.</p>
      <p>These are not fringe products. Otter.ai reported $100 million in annual recurring revenue and 35 million users by late 2025, according to the company's own press release. Fireflies claims, per its August 2026 press release, that teams at 75 percent of Fortune 500 companies use the product. (The phrasing "teams at" is the company's — it could mean one team at each company.) Granola raised $125 million at a $1.5 billion valuation in March 2026, per TechCrunch.</p>
      <p>Legal analysts have identified the broader risk category, though none of their published analyses discuss the Waninger case specifically. Littler Mendelson, in a February 2026 article by attorneys Zoe M. Argento and Bradford J. Kelley, identified AI notetaker default behaviors as an employer blind spot, noting discrimination and disparate-impact risks including potential systemic inaccuracies affecting individuals with accents or speech impediments. Mayer Brown, in a June 2026 analysis, identified privilege-waiver and admissibility risks created by AI notetaker outputs. Both analyses predate the Waninger filing by months.</p>
